         alpha(a*b) >= alpha(a)+alpha(b)  (<=> Erdos product bound).
==============================================================================
  alpha( 4) =    5/2  vs alpha(2)+alpha(2) =      2   [ok]
  alpha( 6) =      3  vs alpha(2)+alpha(3) =      3   [TIGHT]
  alpha( 8) =    7/2  vs alpha(2)+alpha(4) =    7/2   [TIGHT]
  alpha(10) =      4  vs alpha(2)+alpha(5) =   19/5   [ok]
  alpha(12) =    9/2  vs alpha(2)+alpha(6) =      4   [ok]
  alpha(14) =   33/7  vs alpha(2)+alpha(7) =   31/7   [ok]
  alpha(16) =   41/8  vs alpha(2)+alpha(8) =    9/2   [ok]
  alpha(18) =   46/9  vs alpha(2)+alpha(9) =      5   [ok]
  alpha(20) =   27/5  vs alpha(2)+alpha(10) =      5   [ok]
  alpha( 9) =      4  vs alpha(3)+alpha(3) =      4   [TIGHT]
  alpha(12) =    9/2  vs alpha(3)+alpha(4) =    9/2   [TIGHT]
  alpha(15) =  74/15  vs alpha(3)+alpha(5) =   24/5   [ok]
  alpha(18) =   46/9  vs alpha(3)+alpha(6) =      5   [ok]
  alpha(21) =   38/7  vs alpha(3)+alpha(7) =   38/7   [TIGHT]
  superadditivity violations over all a*b<=21: 0  (expect 0)

Principal product line  N = 3^j  (K3^[]j, the j-fold triangle product):
 j  N=3^j   2j avgdeg K3^[]j                 note
 1      3    2             2                u(3)=3, alpha=2
 2      9    4             4               u(9)=18, alpha=4
 3     27    6             6 realizable LB u(27)>=81=(6/2)*27
 4     81    8             8 realizable LB u(81)>=324=(8/2)*81
  => alpha(3^j)=2j crosses kappa=6 EXACTLY at j=3 (N=27): the principal
     product line is TANGENT to the kissing threshold at the cube 3^3.
     N=81=3^4 gives alpha>=8>6 (K3^[]4 is a planar UD graph, 8-regular):
     products DO eventually beat 3N, but only at >=32 (THM-433-E), while
     the IRREDUCIBLE crossover N* is at <=28 -- earlier than any product.

==============================================================================
PART D.  Exact K3^[]3 in Q(sqrt3): unit count, regularity, and the
         'H(3,3)+1' probe (max # of the 27 pts on a common unit circle).
==============================================================================
distinct points realized: 27  (want 27)
unit distances (edges): 81  (want 81 = 3*27)
degree sequence: {6: 27}  (want all 6 => 6-regular)
3*N = 81; alpha = 6  (TIE at kappa=6)

H(3,3)+1 probe (EXACT, circumcircle method):
  unit-circle centers coinciding with an existing vertex, deg 6: 27  (these are the 27 hexagon centers = the points
     themselves -- a NEW 28th point CANNOT sit here)
  NEW (off-vertex) unit-circle centers through >=3 pts: 0
  their degree distribution: {}
  => MAX degree of a genuinely NEW added 28th point = 0
  => H(3,3)+1 new pt gives only u(28) >= 81 (< 85=Engel).
     The generic K3^[]3 has NO off-vertex point unit-distant from >=4
     of its 27 vertices: every full hexagon is centered ON a vertex.
     ==> the n=28 crosser is NOT 'H(3,3) + one boundary point'; it is
     a genuinely DIFFERENT irreducible blob (consistent with THM-433).
DONE.
